//! core/proc — ending the process.
//!
//! One function, because `Proc` declares one operation. Exiting gets its own
//! module rather than a line in `core/io` for the same reason the effects are
//! split at all: exiting is a different authority from printing, and a reader
//! of a context can see which of them a program was given.
//!
//! The name is `exit`, not `exitWith`. `exitWith` reads as a method, and the
//! call is `proc.exit(ctx, 3)`.
//!
//! LINUX, MACOS and JS grant it. WEB does not: a page has no process to exit,
//! and a mounted interface stays live.

from "core/effect" import { Proc };

/// Ends the process with `code`, immediately.
///
/// It never returns. No signature can say that, so the type says `()` and the
/// function never produces one. Nothing after the call runs.
///
/// **A `main` that ends normally should return rather than call this.**
/// Returning `.Ok(())` exits 0, and `.Err(msg)` prints `msg` and exits 1. That
/// covers the ordinary cases. Reach for `exit` when a program has to name a
/// *particular* status — a tool whose exit code is part of its interface.
export fn exit<C: Proc>(ctx: C, code: Int): () {
    ctx.exitWith(code)
}
